  <content>
    <section class="practice" id="sect-00001">
      <para id="para-00001">Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
        <exercise id="db3e5236-c2a2-49c7-804d-f43ce4d36a8b" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00001">
            <para id="para-00002">Enter the value of \(a\).</para>
            <para id="para-00003">The quadratic equation \(x^2+7x+10=0\) is in the form of \(ax^2+bx+c=0\).</para>
            <para id="para-00004">What is the value of \(a\)?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00001">
            <para id="para-00005">Correct! The value of \(a\) is 1.</para>
            <para id="para-00006">Try again. Revisit 9.8.1.</para>
            <para id="para-00007">The correct answer is 1. The value of \(a\) is 1.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="acb22ad4-e36a-4c14-b252-8214e407a7d2" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00002">
            <para id="para-00008">Enter the value of \(b\).</para>
            <para id="para-00009">What is the value of \(b\)?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00002">
            <para id="para-00010">Correct! The value of \(b\) is 7.</para>
            <para id="para-00011">Try again. Revisit 9.8.1.</para>
            <para id="para-00012">The correct answer is 7. The value of \(b\) is 7.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_78372383-13cd-404a-8b99-0cb68702154a" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00003">
            <para id="para-00013">Enter the value of \(c\).</para>
            <para id="para-00014">What is the value of \(c\)?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00003">
            <para id="para-00015">Correct! The value of \(c\) is 10.</para>
            <para id="para-00016">Try again. Revisit 9.8.1.</para>
            <para id="para-00017">The correct answer is 10. The value of \(c\) is 10.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_0d72bfb3-3bc6-42ee-8980-b3f3663afc70" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00004">
            <para id="para-00018">Examine the solution method that has been started below and use it to answer parts d and e.</para>
            <para id="para-00019">Original equation \(x^2+7x+10=0\)</para>
            <para id="para-00020"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Subtract 10 from each side. \(x^2+7x=-10\)</para>
            <para id="para-00021"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Multiply each side by 4. \(4x^2+4(7x)=-4(10)\)</para>
            <para id="para-00022"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Rewrite \(4x^2\) as \((2x)^2\) and \(4(7x)\) as \(2(7)2x\). \((2x)^2+2(7)(2x) +\_\_\_\_^2=\_\_\_\_^2-4(10)\)</para>
            <para id="para-00023"><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - \(\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\) \((2x+\_\_\_\_)^2=\_\_\_\_^2-4(10)\)</para>
            <para id="para-00024"><emphasis>Step 5</emphasis> - \(\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\) \(2x+\_\_\_\_= \pm \sqrt{\_\_\_\_^2-4(10)}\)</para>
            <para id="para-00025"><emphasis>Step 6</emphasis> - \(\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\) \(2x=\_\_\_\_\pm \sqrt{\_\_\_\_^2-4(10)}\)</para>
            <para id="para-00026"><emphasis>Step 7</emphasis> - \(\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\) \(x= \_\_\_\_\)</para>
            <para id="para-00027">In Step 2, what might be a good reason for multiplying each side of the equation by 4?</para>
            <para id="para-00028">Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para id="para-00029">You must always multiply both sides by 4 when completing the square.</para>
            <para id="para-00030">If the value of \(c\) is positive and you multiply by 4, it will be easier to divide the \(b\) term by 3 before you square it.</para>
            <para id="para-00031">Multiplying by 4 makes the coefficient of the \(b\) term a perfect square, which makes it easier to complete the square.</para>
            <para id="para-00032">Multiplying by 4 makes the coefficient of the squared term a perfect square, which makes it easier to complete the square.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00004">
            <para id="para-00033">Correct! Multiplying by 4 makes the coefficient of the squared term a perfect square, which makes it easier to complete the square.</para>
            <para id="para-00034">Try again. Revisit activities 9.8.1 and 9.8.2.</para>
            <para id="para-00035">The correct answer is: Multiplying by 4 makes the coefficient of the squared term a perfect square, which makes it easier to complete the square.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="a625e32e-f588-4181-a0ab-9075ea8737d1" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00005">
            <para id="para-00036">Complete the unfinished steps, then <emphasis>select two</emphasis> solutions.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00005">
            <para id="para-00037">Correct! The solutions are -5 and -2.</para>
            <para id="para-00038">Here are the unfinished steps:</para>
            <para id="para-00039"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Rewrite \(4x^2\) as \((2x)^2\) and \(4(7x)\) as \(2(7)2x\). - Rewrite \(4x^2\) as \((2x)^2\) and \(4(7x)\) as \(2(7)2x\). \((2x)^2+2(7)(2x) +(7)^2=(7)^2-4(10)\) \((2x)^2+2(7)(2x) +(7)^2=(7)^2-4(10)\)</para>
            <para id="para-00040"><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Factor the trinomial. - Factor the trinomial. \((2x+7)^2=(7)^2-4(10)\) \((2x+7)^2=(7)^2-4(10)\)</para>
            <para id="para-00041"><emphasis>Step 5</emphasis> - Take the square root of each side. - Take the square root of each side. \(2x+7= \pm \sqrt{7^2-4(10)}\) \(2x+7= \pm \sqrt{7^2-4(10)}\)</para>
            <para id="para-00042"><emphasis>Step 6</emphasis> - Subtract 7 from both sides. - Subtract 7 from both sides. \(2x=-7 \pm \sqrt{7^2-4(10)}\) \(2x=-7 \pm \sqrt{7^2-4(10)}\)</para>
            <para id="para-00043"><emphasis>Step 7</emphasis> - Solve. - Solve. \(x=-5\), -2 \(x=-5\), -2</para>
            <para id="para-00044">Try again. Revisit activities 9.8.1 and 9.8.2.</para>
            <para id="para-00045">The correct answers are: -5 and -2.</para>
            <para id="para-00046">Here are the unfinished steps:</para>
            <para id="para-00047"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Rewrite \(4x^2\) as \((2x)^2\) and \(4(7x)\) as \(2(7)2x\). - Rewrite \(4x^2\) as \((2x)^2\) and \(4(7x)\) as \(2(7)2x\). \((2x)^2+2(7)(2x) +(7)^2=(7)^2-4(10)\) \((2x)^2+2(7)(2x) +(7)^2=(7)^2-4(10)\)</para>
            <para id="para-00048"><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Factor the trinomial. - Factor the trinomial. \((2x+7)^2=(7)^2-4(10)\) \((2x+7)^2=(7)^2-4(10)\)</para>
            <para id="para-00049"><emphasis>Step 5</emphasis> - Take the square root of each side. - Take the square root of each side. \(2x+7= \pm \sqrt{7^2-4(10)}\) \(2x+7= \pm \sqrt{7^2-4(10)}\)</para>
            <para id="para-00050"><emphasis>Step 6</emphasis> - Subtract 7 from both sides. - Subtract 7 from both sides. \(2x=-7 \pm \sqrt{7^2-4(10)}\) \(2x=-7 \pm \sqrt{7^2-4(10)}\)</para>
            <para id="para-00051"><emphasis>Step 7</emphasis> - Solve. - Solve. \(x=-5\), -2 \(x=-5\), -2</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_1f83ae39-a00f-4bd9-8b72-550d1843c7a6" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00006">
            <para id="para-00052">Substitute the values of \(a\), \(b\), and \(c\) into the quadratic formula, \(x=\frac{-b \pm \sqrt{b^2- 4ac}}{2a}\), but do not evaluate any of the expressions. Explain how this expression is related to solving \(x^2+7x+10=0\) by completing the square.</para>
            <para id="para-00053">Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para id="para-00054">In both forms of solving the equation, you need to list the values of \(a\), \(b\), and \(c\) to determine the solutions.</para>
            <para id="para-00055">Rather than evaluating at each step, the calculation is done all at once, at the end.</para>
            <para id="para-00056">They are related because neither one can have a negative \(c\) value.</para>
            <para id="para-00057">For this equation, the quadratic formula and completing the square are not the same and therefore not related.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00006">
            <para id="para-00058">Correct! Rather than evaluating at each step, the calculation is done all at once, at the end.</para>
            <para id="para-00059">Try again. Revisit activities 9.8.1 and 9.8.2.</para>
            <para id="para-00060">The correct answer is: Rather than evaluating at each step, the calculation is done all at once, at the end.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="f30fb96e-9c43-4d25-8acb-2c705204e8eb" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00007">
            <para id="para-00061">Enter the value of \(a\).</para>
            <para id="para-00062">Consider the standard form of the equation \(x^2−39=0\).</para>
            <para id="para-00063">What is the value of \(a\)?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00007">
            <para id="para-00064">Correct! The value of \(a\) is 1.</para>
            <para id="para-00065">Try again. Revisit 9.8.1.</para>
            <para id="para-00066">The correct answer is 1. The value of \(a\) is 1.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_9310921f-1e37-4cb5-894d-00b995a9df75" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00008">
            <para id="para-00067">Enter the value of \(b\).</para>
            <para id="para-00068">What is the value of \(b\)?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00008">
            <para id="para-00069">Correct! The value of \(b\) is 0.</para>
            <para id="para-00070">Try again. Revisit 9.8.1.</para>
            <para id="para-00071">The correct answer is 0. The value of \(b\) is 0.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_44da0935-583a-49f8-8fbf-42395ea81a40" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00009">
            <para id="para-00072">Enter the value of \(c\).</para>
            <para id="para-00073">What is the value of \(c\)?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00009">
            <para id="para-00074">Correct! The value of \(c\) is -39.</para>
            <para id="para-00075">Try again. Revisit 9.8.1.</para>
            <para id="para-00076">The correct answer is -39. The value of \(c\) is -39.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_69305262-192e-44aa-bd03-412bc5c66070" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00010">
            <para id="para-00077">Can you use the quadratic formula to solve this equation?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00010">
            <para id="para-00078">Correct! The quadratic formula works for all quadratic equations. Substituting the values of \(a\), \(b\), and \(c\) into the quadratic formula and evaluating the expression gives the solutions.</para>
            <para id="para-00079">Try again. Revisit activities 9.8.1 – 9.8.3.</para>
            <para id="para-00080">The correct answer is yes. The quadratic formula works for all quadratic equations. Substituting the values of \(a\), \(b\), and \(c\) into the quadratic formula and evaluating the expression gives the solutions.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_61fb1551-01ea-42b2-ac7d-a9bb6e748245" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00011">
            <para id="para-00081">Can you solve this equation using square roots?</para>
            <para id="para-00082">Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para id="para-00083">Yes, the solutions are \(\pm 39\).</para>
            <para id="para-00084">No, there is no solution.</para>
            <para id="para-00085">Yes, the solutions are \(\pm \sqrt{39}\).</para>
            <para id="para-00086">No, this equation can only be solved by the quadratic formula.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00011">
            <para id="para-00087">Correct! Yes, adding 39 to both sides of the equation gives \(x^2=39\). The solutions are \(\pm \sqrt{39}\).</para>
            <para id="para-00088">Try again. Revisit activities 9.8.1 and 9.8.2.</para>
            <para id="para-00089">The correct answer is: Yes, adding 39 to both sides of the equation gives \(x^2=39\). The solutions are \(\pm \sqrt{39}\).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_8f682d62-d494-473f-8d61-49e01216ec73" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00012">
            <para id="para-00090">She arrived at this equation: \((2ax+b)^2=b^2−4ac\).</para>
            <para id="para-00091">Clare is deriving the quadratic formula by solving \(ax^2+bx+c=0\) by completing the square.</para>
            <para id="para-00092">Choose the best description of what she needs to do to finish solving for \(x\).</para>
            <para id="para-00093">Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para id="para-00094">Subtract \(b^2\) from each side. Find the square roots of each side. Then divide each side by 2.</para>
            <para id="para-00095">Find the square roots of each side. Subtract \(b \) from each side. Then divide each side by \(2a\).</para>
            <para id="para-00096">Divide each side by 2. Subtract \(b \) from each side. Then find the square roots of each side.</para>
            <para id="para-00097">Find the square roots of each side. Divide each side by 2. Subtract \(b \) from each side.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00012">
            <para id="para-00098">Correct! She needs to find the square roots of each side. Subtract \(b \) from each side. Then divide each side by \(2a\).</para>
            <para id="para-00099">Try again. Revisit activities 9.8.1 – 9.8.3.</para>
            <para id="para-00100">The correct answer is: She needs to find the square roots of each side. Subtract \(b \) from each side. Then divide each side by \(2a\).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
    </section>
  </content>
